lly  or you can have a total for the thc and  the total of cbd  and you can customize these reports but  that's the nice thing is you have a  dedicated instrument that does the  analysis and then gives you  a nice concise data report  now there are three modes of operation  there is one it's the  uh the high sensitive the high  throughput mode  which if you need to really crank  through a lot of uh  samples then there is the high  sensitivity mode which is the one that's  most used in most of the cannabis  labs and then there is the high  resolution one that's really more for  r d purposes so if you are looking at a  new cultivar for instance and you're  trying to see the  the high sensitivity one like i said is  the most popular one because it gives  you a good  uh trace level determination  so four years ago this is what you might  have seen with the high throughput  method  and you know you get ten cannabinoids in  uh  eight minutes with the high sensitivity  min  uh method you get 11 cannabinoids in  10 minutes and with the high resolution  minutes you get 11 cannabinoids in  30 minutes now it's a little longer run  time but you can see that you have  perfect baseline  a resolution of all the cannabinoids now  that was four years ago  since then things have changed the lists  have grown as as you all know but also  some of the technology has evolved so  today  we can do 18 cannabinoids so in the high  resolution minute  a method and then actually do it in a  little bit less time  the high throughput method again we  stayed the same at eight minutes but  we nearly doubled the number of  cannabinoids that can be run in that  same time  and like i said for our most popular one  the high sensitivity method today  it is 21 cannabinoids and it's a little  bit longer but you can see that you can  get very nice  quantitation of these 21 cannabinoids  so the point is that you know four years  ago  compared to today so there are some  changes so  what's nice with the cannabis analyzer  is that you have the software and you  get  updates to the software that then allow  you to  always have the right method for the  latest  regular latest requirements for the  number of cannabinoids  and the other nice thing is you can  actually look at it from your smartphone  you can check the system status the  chromatogram process  progress so from your smartphone you  could be anywhere and  take a look at your analysis  okay now let's move over to our gcms  and now we'll look at terpenes  there are a lot of terpenes in in  cannabis over 200 have been found so far  and there is some synergistic effect  that you get with the cannabinoids it's  often called the  the entourage effect now  terpenes are part of a larger family of  compounds  known as essential oils and essential  oils are used in aromatherapy  so in and by themselves  terpenes already have a health benefit  and if you combine that  with the health benefit of the  cannabinoids you get that really that  holistic cannabis experience by having  the cannabinoids and the  terpenes with their entourage effect  so let's look at this particular  cultivar that has  22 percent thc and then it has  some terpenes there pinene and  caryophylline  and those percentages the 40 and 25  are not off the total flower that's  actually off the  terpene content but the pinene as you  can see here it's got anti-inflammatory  antibacterial  effect so it does offer those additional  health benefits  compare that to this cultivar that's the  same male  different female so you have about the  same  uh thc 24  but you have a different uh terpene  profile in this case you have  mercy which is the the predominant um  terpene and it's a sedative and  anti-insomnia so  it will give you a much more relaxing  experience  than the previous cultivar would have so  the the terpenes play a big role  in how the uh  cannabis the interacts with your with  your body  so for analysis again we use our  headspace gc or gcms  and we can do very nicely this is a list  of i think 38  terpenes that you can see there very  nicely uh  resolved and analyzed and quantitative  okay so moving on now to  uh residual solvents in this case we're  using a  headspace gc  so like many of the analyses  in the cannabis it's a it's a list-based  approach so in this case we're looking  at the oregon list which is about  35 residual solvents and you can see  with our headspace gc we can very easily  separate those 35 residual solvents  and we can even do the uh california  list which is a  20 residual solvents and you can see  here very nicely again excellent  resolution good quantitation  in the picture you actually see a  headspace  ms and you can actually  combine that if you have the headspace  gcms then you can do  the terpenes and the residual solvents  in a single run so you can actually use  the same tool but do two different  types of analysis so you get a two for  one special if you will  okay next we'll move to our icpms which  we will use to analyze for heavy metals  why do we worry about hemming heavy  metals well plants in general  bioaccumulate metals from the soil  and you know hemp in cannabis is really  no  no exception to that and that can be  positive because you can get uh  a good nutrients from the soil and you  can get  good metals if you will uh potassium  iron calcium and things  but at the same time you also get some  of the toxic metals and  in this case we want to analyze them by  icp ms inductively coupled  uh plasma mass spectrometry and usually  when people talk about heavy metals they  talk about the big four  lead cadmium mercury  and arsenic  and the regulatory agencies like the usp  and fda they establish permissible daily  exposure limits  and you can see those here in table one  you know for the arsenic cadmium lid  and mercury and they're in the uh  low uh ppm range  so when we did the analysis of a  cannabis flower  you know we compared that to those  limits on the bottom row now of table  two  you can see those limits from table one  uh for comparison then to the matrix and  you can see in the top row when we look  at matrix  that the levels we found in the cannabis  flower  are actually significantly below  the admissible daily exposure limit so  we don't need to worry about heavy  metals in  that particular cannabis flower  okay now we'll turn to our lcms  triple quad in the front here and first  of all  use it to analyze for pesticides  like any crop  cannabis hat is susceptible to attacks  by  pests so a little bug so people use  pesticides to protect the crop  and then we'll have to analyze for those  pesticides  historically much of the  pesticide analysis was done by gcm sms  but more recently a lot of it has  shifted towards lc  ms triple quad and it has to do with uh  the volatility of the pesticides uh the  polarity of the pesticides  but more pesticides are amenable to elsi  than there  are to to chisel  and in fact we can do hundreds of  pesticides with lc  msms you can see here 211 pesticides  uh very easily done with with an lcms  triple quad the bigger challenge really  is  keeping up with the ever-evolving  pesticide  lists as you can see here now each state  has  has a different list and it started in  colorado and got to oregon at 59 and  then  california said well oregon i'll see  your 59 and i'll race you to 66.  and then canada goes 66 hey well  we can do 96 so canada's starting a list  of 96  pesticides and then currently  aoac international is working on a list  of  104 pesticides and not only  is it a larger list or more compounds  but they're also looking to lower the  limit of quantitation  and the limit of uh residue  residue detection so  the point i'm making is that these lists  are expected to continue to evolve  you know the laboratory capabilities  change instrument capabilities change  uh risk assessments regulations  laws so keep an eye on that rather than  worry about whether the instrument can  handle  instrument can if you have an lc triple  quad ms  it can handle 104 it can handle 204 so  but just make sure that you are aware of  which list you  are running  just like with the gcms with the  headspace we can do  a second analysis with the uh lc-ms  triple quad  in this case we're looking at  microtoxins  so mycotoxins and their carcinogenic  type aflatoxins their type of mold  that is present in a lot of plants  including  cannabis and so there's testing required  for the the four aflatoxins b1  b2 g1 and g2 and then there's also  mycotoxin  oculotoxin a that needs to be analyzed  in most  cases what's nice is as i mentioned  because we already did the pesticides on  the lcms we can do those combined so in  this case you see that we have the  oregon pesticide list  plus the microtoxin plus the aflatoxins  all in a single run now if you need a  little more  sensitivity for the aflatoxins and  microtoxins then  a conventional hplc with the  fluorescence detector will give you a  little better sensitivity down to the  single digit part per billion  range as you can see here in the in the  table where we quantitated the  the data  okay so now let's look at uh moisture  testing  that's our little moisture testing is  sitting there in the corner  mold and bacteria can really thrive  in a moist environment and of course we  don't want that especially in prolonged  storage  and the data have shown that the moles  can really  proliferate when the moisture level  exceeds 12 percent  so with a moisture balance you can very  easily  determine the moisture level and what's  nice about this test too is that it is  non-destructive meaning that you can  do a moisture analysis test and then  take  these the the product and put it back on  the shelf  just have to make sure especially if  you're going to store the product for  any length of time  i have to assure that the total moisture  content  is less than 12 otherwise you uh  could run the danger of seeing some mold  growth  and now for something completely  different brought to you by  the ministry of silly walks  a convenient and tasty  way to enjoy or administer uh  cannabis cannabinoids is through edibles  and they could be you know the brownies  cookies and everything and there  and they ever popular gummy bears now  edibles are then a type of food and  texture  in food is is quite important and  we have a certain perception of texture  and it's based on you know maybe a prior  experience so if you look at the  popcorn you know we have a certain  expectation and we buy popcorn although  it's nuts on the bottom right  i want them to be hard and you know like  a peanut be a little softer than an  almond for instance  when you take a bite of an apple there's  a certain texture that you expect you  don't want it to be mealy and soft but  kind of crisp and juicy  and even when it comes to chocolate you  know a chocolate cake  for instance you want that to be soft  and moist  while a piece of chocolate you want that  to be a little firm  and dry of course and and then of course  the gummies you know you have a certain  expectation on the chewiness  of the gummies so  texture is actually a sensory property  of a food  and there was a scientist dr elena  chechnyak  who developed this texture profile  analysis and so she correlated some of  these  characteristics like hardness  adhesiveness  elastic quality to certain sensory  experiences that someone will have  eating the food  and we can actually quantitate that so  we can do a texture profile analysis and  it's basically  looking at the mechanical properties by  taking a food in this case we have these  samples of edibles the gummies  and you can see in the middle picture  you know you have to come you have the  plunger come down  and then they started to squish the  gummy i mean i i love these instruments  because they're so  fun to watch you can actually see what  what's happening  but we you know we squish the gummy  deform it  and then there is a lot of math and  algorithms that happen  uh behind the scenes and you can then  get quantitative output of  characteristics like  hardness cohesiveness chewiness and you  don't need to worry about any of that  math or anything that's all part of the  software and it does all that behind the  scenes  but then you get a nice quantitative  output and  that's really important if you're  developing a new  edible and you're trying out  new additives new formulations and  this gives you a way to quantify  a number for what you're the consumer  then may  describe it as soft or hard or chewy  so you can see that here and this is  becomes important so  in this case we're looking at two  different brands of medicated gummies  and you can see that brand b has a  higher firmness and chewiness compared  to brand  a so if you think about what that means  is that you have to chew brand b  longer to get it into a consistency  that's suitable for swallowing so it's  the mastication  and excuse me that may not be  as critical if it's safe for  recreational use for adults  but if this is a medicated gummy that's  prescribed to  say a small child then that process of  chewing it and breaking it down to a  consistency where the child can swallow  it  that becomes uh more important and so  it's nice to know that you can  quantitate that  ahead of time as part of your product  development  now this is a test that's not uh  not typical for your cannabis labs but i  just wanted you to be aware of  that there is a instrumentation that  allows you to  quantitate the texture of  the edibles so  which then you know brings me back to  having the right tool for the for the  right job you know there's these  seven analyses they're color-coded here  the ones in  in the dark blue at the top those are  the desirable  constituents ingredients of the cannabis  the cannabinoids and the terpenes  the ones in reds are the ones that you  don't want in there but  you still have to check for them and  then of course you have the moisture at  the end  point being there's a right tool for  any of these type 7 analyses and some of  the tools can be used multiple times  like the lc triple quad ms  pesticides microtoxins or the gcms  for the terpenes and the residual  solvents  so that's uh all i have for today i want  to  thank you for your attention if you have  any questions  feel free to email me at ercoon  shimatsu.com  or you can go to www.growyourlab.com and  there's  you'll find a lot of good information  about cannabis and hemp analysis  on that on that website and with that  again i want to thank you and bye bye

